<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapan is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the capital of Syunik Province, in the valley of the Voghji river. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a fortress and seat of the kings of Syunik, and the area has long been known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines. In the 18th century it was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">Bek</a>'s rising against Persian and Ottoman rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kapan.jpg" alt="Kapan" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See above · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kapan_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kapan lies in the narrow valley of the Voghji River on the southeastern slopes of the Zangezur Mountains, between the Bargushat and Meghri ridges, on the northern slopes of Mount Khustup, which at 3,201 metres is the highest peak of the region. Its elevation is 750 to 1,050 metres. The name comes from the Classical Armenian kapan, meaning mountain pass. It later became Ghap'an, which was in use until 1991, when the older form was restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was first mentioned in the 5th century as a small settlement among the properties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siunia-dynasty">Siunia dynasty</a>, in the Baghk' canton of Syunik, the ninth province of historic Greater Armenia. Prince Dzagik fortified it in the late 9th and early 10th century, and it became the residence of the Dzagikian princes. By the end of the 10th century Prince Smbat II moved to Kapan and founded the Kingdom of Syunik, proclaiming himself king under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the Bagratid Kingdom of Armenia. As its capital Kapan flourished in the 11th century, reaching around 20 thousand inhabitants, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> with a prominent Jewish minority, and was a centre of trade, crafts and metallurgy. </p><p>In 1103 Seljuk invaders ruined it; the historian Stephen Orbelian says they began their massacres in the Jewish quarter. After the kingdom fell in 1170 Syunik suffered Seljuk, Mongol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> invasions between the 12th and 15th centuries.</p><p>In the early 16th century Kapan became part of the Erivan Province of Safavid Iran. In 1722 the noble families of Syunik united in an Armenian principality of Kapan, and the military leader David Bek began the campaign against the Iranians and the invading Ottoman Turks, helped by thousands of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avan-yuzbashi">Avan Yuzbashi</a> and Mkhitar Sparapet. The centre of his struggle was the Baghaberd Fortress northwest of Kapan and the Halidzor Fortress, where he died in 1728. </p><p>In 1813 Syunik became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a>. In the first half of the 19th century Russian and German engineers and workers came to work in the region's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines, and Greek specialists arrived in 1850.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kapan is a town in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the capital of Syunik Province, which was formed in 1995 after Armenia's independence. The town received city status in 1938 and was a major mining and industrial centre of Soviet Armenia, known as Kafan in Russian. It is almost entirely Armenian and its population was 43,190 in 2011. Heritage monuments in the area include the Baghaberd Fortress and a medieval bridge dating from 871.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kapan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from fortresses such as <b>Kapan</b> over a land of deep valleys, mines and pasture.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-syunik">Kingdom of Syunik</a></li></ul></section>
